  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 103 PM MDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nThe drier airmass will continue to spread south and eastward Friday, \nleading to a substantial decrease in storm coverage across most of \nthe state. The best remaining moisture will be confined to the \nwestern border (PWATs ~0.70-0.80 inches), where isolated storms may \ndevelop. Hot and dry conditions are expected across eastern NM, with \ntemperatures potentially approaching or exceeding records in some \nlocations. Moisture will begin returning eastward on Saturday as the \nridge responds to the propagation of a trough across the Pacific NW, \nbringing isolated to scattered afternoon showers and storms back \ninto the forecast for the western and central mountains. \n\nBy Sunday and into early next week, a gradual increase in moisture \nacross the state is expected as southwesterly flow aloft becomes \nmore prevalent due to the positioning of the aforementioned trough \nand the strengthening high over the Southeast. This should support \nan increase in precipitation coverage as we head into next week. \nTemperatures look to trend down in response, closer to seasonal \nnormals, during this period. Although, given we are 5-7 days out, \nuncertainty remains, particularly with regards to how effectively \nthis moisture return plays out.",
